# Language detection on GitHub.
#
# Two independent linguist exclusions have to be turned off before any of the
# languages this plugin exists to emit appears at all, and neither of them is
# what linguist-detectable controls.
#
# Every .capnp, .fbs and .thrift here is committed twice: once under
# examples/generated as the demonstration output, and once under
# plugin/factory/registry/testdata as the golden fixture the tests compare it
# against. linguist's documentation.yml matches `^[Ee]xamples/` and its vendor.yml
# matches `(^|/)testdata/`, so *both* copies are dropped from the language
# statistics before detectability is ever consulted. That is why the sidebar
# showed no Cap'n Proto, no FlatBuffers and no Thrift rather than a small slice of
# each — with the files split evenly across two excluded trees, the count was
# zero.
#
# The examples copy is the one to count: it is the output, and it is what someone
# is looking at when they want to know what this produces. The testdata copy
# stays vendored — it is the same fifteen files, and counting a golden fixture as
# source would double every number for no added truth.
examples/** linguist-documentation=false

# Detectability is a separate, per-language default: linguist counts `programming`
# and `markup` languages and ignores `data` ones. Of the four below, Cap'n Proto
# and Thrift are `programming`, so those two lines are belt and braces while the
# other two are load-bearing — .proto is linguist's `Proto` (data) and .fbs its
# `FlatBuffers` (data), and without these they stay at zero even inside a counted
# directory.
#
# The belt-and-braces lines are kept rather than trimmed because the cost is a
# line each and the failure they cover is silent: a linguist release that
# reclassifies either language would drop it from the sidebar with nothing here
# changing and no error anywhere.
*.proto  linguist-detectable=true
*.fbs    linguist-detectable=true
*.capnp  linguist-detectable=true
*.thrift linguist-detectable=true

# .msg and .srv are ROS interface definitions. linguist has no id for them, and a
# value with a space in it is not expressible here at all — git splits this file
# on whitespace and does not honour quoting — so they are simply marked as
# generated, which is what they are.
*.msg    linguist-generated=true
*.srv    linguist-generated=true

# Everything below is compared byte-for-byte by the test suite, so git must not
# rewrite it on checkout. Without these, git's default core.autocrlf=true on
# Windows turns every committed LF into CRLF and the comparison fails on the line
# ending alone.
#
# The .proto *inputs* are pinned as well as the expected outputs, and not only
# because the golden harness reads them: doc comments are lifted out of proto
# source and forwarded into every emitted schema, so a CRLF checkout of an input
# file can put a stray \r in the middle of a golden line rather than only at the
# end of one — which reads as a content mismatch, not a line-ending one.
*.proto text eol=lf

# Golden fixtures: case inputs and expected output trees. The glob is deliberate
# rather than a list of extensions — .thrift joined .fbs and .capnp here without
# this file needing a line, and the next target will too.
plugin/factory/registry/testdata/** text eol=lf

# Committed generated output. Not gated by CI, but `just regen` rewrites it with
# LF, so a CRLF checkout would show every line of every file as modified.
examples/generated/** text eol=lf

# buffers.lock is the ordinal ledger. It is compared, never merged: a conflict in
# it is a real conflict about wire compatibility and must be resolved by a human.
buffers.lock -merge
